WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.260
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.340
An evil warden holds you prisoner,

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.880
but offers you a chance
to earn your freedom.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.300
You are given 101 coins,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.080
of which 51 are genuine
and 50 are counterfeit.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.600
Each counterfeit coin is identical
to a genuine coin,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.120
except that it differs in weight
by exactly one gram.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.940
All of the counterfeit coins are lighter,
or all of them are heavier.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.800
Only the warden knows
this information.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.480
The warden gives you a 
randomly selected coin

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.200
from the 101 coins.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.500
You have to guess whether it is
genuine or counterfeit.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.900
If you are incorrect,
you are imprisoned forever,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.900
but if you are correct,
then you are set free.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.700
You are given a tool
to help.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.100
You are given a weighing balance

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.500
that displays the difference in weight

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.960
between its left and right pans.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.100
For example,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.080
if the left pan has 8.3 grams

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.520
and the right pan has 10.3 grams,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.600
the display would show
negative two grams.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.900
You can weigh any of
the 101 coins,

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.100
but you only get to use
the weighing balance once

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.200
before you have to guess.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.600
What is your best strategy
to identify your coin

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.180
and escape the prison?

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.480
Can you figure it out?

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.400
Give this problem a try,
and when you're ready,

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.720
keep watching the video
for the solution.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.640
It seems impossible
because you're at a huge disadvantage.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.660
You don't know the weight
of a genuine coin

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.840
and you don't know
if the counterfeit coin

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.340
is lighter or heavier.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.040
Plus, the weighing balance
only gives

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.100
the difference in weight
between its left and right pans.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.920
But amazingly,
you can always escape.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.180
And remarkably,
you don't actually weigh

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.100
the coin you need
to identify,

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.880
you end up weighing
all of the other coins.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.360
Here's how it works.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.140
You place your coin aside.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.280
Then you split the remaining
100 coins

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.220
into left and right pans

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.740
with 50 coins
in each pan.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.460
Now the display on the balance

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.900
will always be an integer.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.020
I'll explain why
later in the video.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.800
If the integer is even,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.380
then you initially received
a genuine coin.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.560
If the integer is odd,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.400
then you initially received
a counterfeit coin.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.680
By a clever mathematical scheme,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.060
you can outsmart the evil warden

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.080
and guarantee your freedom.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.340
This will always let you identify

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.640
whether your coin is genuine
or counterfeit,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:07.840
but why does it work?

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.320
Let's go through a proof.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.300
Let w equal the weight
of a genuine coin

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.000
and let c sub i equal the weight
of coin i.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.440
We'll number the coins
in the left pan

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.080
from 1 to 50,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.620
and we'll number the coins
in the right pan

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.300
from 51 to 100.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.400
We can then write the difference

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.000
of the weight in the coins

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.480
on the left hand pan

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.980
from the right hand pan

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.460
as equaling the sum of the coins
in the left pan

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.340
minus the sum of the coins
in the right pan.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.340
We now are going
to introduce

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.120
the weight of a genuine coin.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.520
We'll start out with the
simple expression

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.240
that zero is equal to 
negative 50 w

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.880
minus negative 50 w.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.960
We will then expand out
each term

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.720
so that we have zero equaling

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.000
negative w fifty times

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.800
minus negative w fifty times.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.380
We can now add up
these equations.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:21.720
The reason we're doing this

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.480
is we're going to pair
the weight of each coin

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.660
with the weight
of a genuine coin.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.180
So we have a result
that left minus right

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.160
is equal to the sum

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.900
of the weight of each coin

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.580
minus the weight
of a genuine coin.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.900
We have one hundred
of these terms

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.660
consisting of fifty terms

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.940
that are added on the left pan

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.940
and fifty terms
which are subtracted

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.280
from the right pan.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.480
Now we know something

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.000
about each of these terms.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.880
For a genuine coin,

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.720
its weight is exactly equal

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.520
to the weight of a genuine coin,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.960
so each of these terms
will equal zero

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.380
and they'll vanish.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.340
If all of the counterfeit coins
are heavier,

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.820
then the weight
of a counterfeit coin

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.040
minus the weight
of a genuine coin

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.340
will equal 1.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.160
If instead all of the counterfeits
are lighter,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.320
then the weight of a counterfeit coin

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.560
minus the weight
of a genuine coin

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.460
will equal minus 1.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.860
We'll consider each of
these cases separately.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.800
So let's continue the proof
by considering

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.360
if all of the counterfeit
coins are heavier.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.660
So how many counterfeit coins
are there?

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.860
We will either be weighing
50 counterfeit coins

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.180
if we received a genuine coin,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.500
or we will be weighing
49 counterfeit coins

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.420
if we received a counterfeit.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.580
So we'll consider
each of these cases.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:01.440
Suppose that we are weighing
50 counterfeit coins,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.760
and we're placing x of them
on the left pan

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:07.540
and 50 minus x
on the right pan.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.440
Each of these counterfeit coins

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.260
will contribute one

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.880
to this
left minus right.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.620
So we have
left minus right equals

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.480
x from the left pan

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.220
minus 
50 minus x

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.280
from the right pan.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.600
This simplifies to be
2x minus 50,

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.460
and what's important is that
2x is even,

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.460
50 is even,
and the difference

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.400
of two even terms
is even.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.320
If instead there were
49 counterfeit coins,

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.580
suppose we placed
x on the left pan

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.040
and 49 minus x 
on the right pan.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.000
We can again calculate
that left minus right

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.700
will equal x minus
49 minus x,

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.240
and this simplifies
to be 2x minus 49.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.780
The only difference here
is that 49 is an odd number.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.480
We have an even number, 2x,
minus an odd number,

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.400
which will therefore give us
an odd result.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.140
So what we've figured out is that

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:21.580
the even-ness and odd-ness
of our result will depend

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.180
on whether we're weighing 
50 or 49 coins

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:26.500
that are counterfeit.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.260
This will also be true

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:32.000
if the counterfeits are all lighter.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.080
It's a very similar calculation.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:38.120
If we ended up weighing 
50 counterfeit coins,

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.400
we'll end up with an even result,

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.040
and if we were weighing
49 counterfeit coins,

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.560
we would end up
with an odd result.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.760
So we can generalize
all of these cases together

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.280
whether we're dealing with heavier
or lighter counterfeit coins.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:58.200
If we end up with the balance
showing an even integer,

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.840
then that means
we would have weighed

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.120
all 50 of the counterfeit coins,

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:03.740
so that means initially

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.440
we would have received
one of the genuine coins.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:12.200
If instead the balance is showing
an odd number,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.400
that means
we would have been weighing

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.400
49 of the counterfeit coins,

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.600
so initially we would have received

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.340
one of those counterfeit coins.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:25.100
Therefore, with certainty,
we can figure out,

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.760
from what the weighing balance 
is showing,

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:29.120
whether the coin we had

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.300
is genuine or counterfeit.

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.020
Did you figure this out?

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.040
Thanks for watching this video.

